  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Above average temperatures expected through the \nperiod.\n\nAbove average temperatures expected through the period.  An \nupper level ridge and surface high pressure will be over the \nregion through the middle portions of the week. 850mb \ntemperatures are expected to reside in the +14C to +16C range \nfor much of the area. These values place much of the area within\nthe 90 to 97.5 percentile of the 30-year CFSR climatology mid-\nweek through the weekend. While a cold front is expected to \ncross the area Thursday, limited change in the thermal profile \nis expected. Above normal temperatures are expected, with \nSaturday currently looking like the coolest of the seven days.\n\nKey Message 2: Next good rain chance starting Thursday, continuing \nthrough at least the weekend.\n\nHigh pressure will provide for a dry forecast through Wednesday. A \ncold front is expected to cross the region Thursday through \nThursday night then stall to our south. Upstream, another \ndisturbances is expected to cross the Great Lakes region by the \nweekend. These features will keep our weather pattern active with \ndaily chance of showers and storms starting on Thursday, lasting \ninto the weekend. While the situation will not be a a continuous rain \nwashout across the region, some locally heavy rain will be possible \nwith some of this activity. On Thursday, northwestern sections of \nthe area may see more abundance of rain than southeastern areas. In \nfact, depending upon the speed of the front, areas such as Danville \nmay need to wait until late Thursday night into Friday to see \nprecipitation.",
